<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drago Doctrine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Drago Doctrine was the principle, announced in 1902 by the Argentine foreign minister Luis María Drago, that no foreign power may use armed force to collect public debts from an American state. It was a response to the naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Venezuela by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and a modified form was adopted at the Hague Peace Conference of 1907.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drago-doctrine.jpg" alt="Drago Doctrine" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis_Mar%C3%ADa_Drago.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The doctrine grew from the ideas of Carlos Calvo in his Derecho internacional teórico y práctico de Europa y América, known as the Calvo Doctrine, which proposed to forbid diplomatic intervention before local remedies were exhausted. Its occasion was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Venezuela in 1902 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the Venezuelan government refused to pay a large foreign debt run up by administrations before President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro">Cipriano Castro</a> took power. Drago set out his principle in a diplomatic note to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Drago held that simply failing to repay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> was no valid reason for foreign intervention, above all by a power outside the Western Hemisphere. He was answering a tension between the Monroe Doctrine, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> out, and European demands for repayment, and he assumed the principle of sovereign equality that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> had long supported. The doctrine stated explicitly that no foreign power, the United States included, could use force against a nation of the Western Hemisphere to collect government debts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Secretary of State John Hay, taken aback by the reference to the Monroe Doctrine, waited six weeks before replying with a line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>'s annual message of 1901: &quot;We do not guarantee any state against punishment if it misconducts itself.&quot; Roosevelt later praised the doctrine, though as Vice President he had written to the German diplomat Hermann Speck von Sternburg that a misbehaving South American state should be spanked by the European country it wronged.</p><p>In 1904 the Roosevelt Corollary answered the doctrine by asserting a right of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, and to forestall European interventions the United States took control of the customs of several Latin American countries to ensure that debts were paid. The modified version adopted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1907, known as the Porter Convention after Horace Porter, added that arbitration and litigation should always come first. Venezuela later cited the doctrine for its vote supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> over Argentina's debt dispute with NML Capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drago Doctrine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Argentine diplomacy produced the <b>Drago Doctrine</b> of 1902 against the forcible collection of public debts, and Carlos Saavedra Lamas won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1936 for mediating the end of the Chaco War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
